            <ArticleTitle>Copper(II)-Controlled Molecular Glue for Mismatched DNA.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>618-622</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1002/cbic.201600675</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Isothermal hybridization of two DNA strands bearing three thymine-thymine (T:T) mismatches can be brought about in the presence of a stoichiometric amount of a bis-naphthalene macrocycle, 2,7-BisNP-NH. This process can be reverted by addition of a CuII salt due to formation of a dinuclear metal complex which does not bind to DNA. Subsequent sequestration of CuII releases the macrocycle and restores the hybridization state of DNA strands, thus allowing implementation of a fast fluorescent two-state DNA switch.</AbstractText>
